Found while doing #40, not fixed there -- one ticket, one change. Last of the four wrappers.

lib/Crypt/Age.pm, =method encrypt_file: its whole "Dies if" clause is "Dies on error (file not found, permission denied, etc)". No enumeration, so it documents neither the recipients ArrayRef shape/emptiness requirement nor any of the other failure modes its three siblings now list.

State of the four wrappers after #38 and #40:

  encrypt_filehandle  -- enumerates, points at L</encrypt>   OK
  decrypt_filehandle  -- enumerates, points at L</decrypt>   fixed in #38
  decrypt_file        -- enumerates, points at L</decrypt>   fixed in #40
  encrypt_file        -- "Dies on error (...)"               this ticket

So this closes the surface that #29, #38 and #40 each cut a piece off: several descriptions of one failure surface disagreeing on length.

Fix shape: give it the same construction the other three now share -- enumerate what it actually dies on and point at L</encrypt>, where the recipients messages are quoted, rather than carrying a copy. Read the live guards in the encrypt path first and document what the code does today, not what this body claims; a documented cryptographic assurance callers make security decisions from must be true of the current code.

Pure POD, no behaviour. prove -lr t/ must stay green.
